WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
In February I posted on my community tab that&nbsp;
YouTube is not promoting my new videos and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.120
people were like, “well of course it is not, you&nbsp;
should do more interesting topics what are these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.760
snoosfests on Beer history, or The Avar Siege&nbsp;
of Constantinople.” Well how’s this for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:21.040
interesting topic, in 1184 in a city called Erfurt&nbsp;
around 60 to 80 nobleman and clergymen drowned in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.800
human excrements while attending a meeting with&nbsp;
the King of Germany. Well as always there’s more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.600
to the story than just that. But let me cut to the&nbsp;
chase, unlike other fun fact videos I have done on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.360
my channel, this one actually happened, but to&nbsp;
understand how and why it happened we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.640
delve into the complicated world of 12th century&nbsp;
nobility politics of the Holy Roman Empire.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.200
Let's first look at Erfurt.
Erfurt was a very important city in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.240
the Holy Roman Empire. It dated back to the 8th&nbsp;
century and possibly, according to tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.280
it had even earlier origins around the 5th&nbsp;
century connected to the Germanic Thuringii&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.080
tribe. The city gained large prominence&nbsp;
when Charlemagne himself designated Erfurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.000
in 805 as a border trading city with the&nbsp;
Slavs in the east. Then as the Frankish power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.800
expanded more eastward and the Frankish&nbsp;
Empire changed to the Holy Roman Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.240
Erfurt no longer was a border trading city but&nbsp;
it still retained its exclusive trading rights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.440
This trading was helped by the fact that&nbsp;
Erfurt laid on one of the Imperial Roads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.920
The Via Regia. The Imperial roads were toll roads&nbsp;
within the Holy Roman Empire where your protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.680
was guaranteed by the Emperor himself, for a&nbsp;
fee. This meant that Erfurt enjoyed a steady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.480
stream of traders traveling along this&nbsp;
road in the early and high middle ages.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.680
The city also housed a royal&nbsp;
palace on Petersberg hill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.880
Both the Carolingian and Ottonian dynasties&nbsp;
used Erfurt as one of their seats of power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.160
For example it was here that Henry the Flower&nbsp;
appointed Otto the first as the sole successor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.360
to East Frankia later to&nbsp;
become the Holy Roman Empire.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.000
However, this very important city fell under&nbsp;
the control of the Archbishopric of Mainz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.040
somewhere around the 10th and 11th century. We&nbsp;
don’t know exactly how or when this happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.760
but most likely it came about after the fall&nbsp;
of the Ottonian dynasty and the rise of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.800
Salian dynasty in the early 11th century. The&nbsp;
Salians didn’t continue with the tradition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.600
having a royal seat in Erfurt and therefore&nbsp;
the city most likely fell into the control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.160
of the powerful Archbishopric of Mainz.&nbsp;
The Salian dynasty later got replaced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.480
the Hohenstaufen Dynasty in the early&nbsp;
12th century, but more on them later.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.680
The fact that the Archbishopric of Mainz ended&nbsp;
up controlling such an important city that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.560
located in the middle of a Duchy called Thuringia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.520
didn’t sit well with the Thuringian ruling&nbsp;
dynasty, the Ludovingians. The Archbishopric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.000
of Mainz also controlled several other areas&nbsp;
near and around Thuringia which meant that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.720
Ludovingians have always been contending with the&nbsp;
Archbishopric of Mainz for control in central HRE.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.200
Of course both of these entities were within&nbsp;
the same state, the Holy Roman Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.800
but the empire became more and more decentralized&nbsp;
ever since the Ottonian dynasty and so fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.440
among the various duchies, principalities&nbsp;
and bishoprics became increasingly common.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.280
Now that we understand the political&nbsp;
setting let's look at the three figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
that made the Erfurt meeting possible&nbsp;
or a better word would be necessary.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.840
From my Guelphs vs Ghibelines video or from&nbsp;
the far more popular oversimplified video on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.080
the war of the bucket, you are probably aware&nbsp;
of the centuries-long struggle between the Holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.760
Roman Emperors and the Papacy. The main part of&nbsp;
this very large and often vague and complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.720
struggle we need to understand at the moment is&nbsp;
the fact that it wasn’t clear whether the Bishops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.760
within the Holy Roman Empire could be appointed&nbsp;
by the emperor or by the Pope. This was a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.160
deal because some bishoprics and archbishoprics&nbsp;
within the HRE like Mainz, for example, were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.680
powerful, rich and controlled a lot of land, so&nbsp;
whoever could appoint the guy who controlled this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.960
land would in essence have far more power. It&nbsp;
also wasn’t clear whether the Pope himself had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.240
to be appointed by the Emperor to officially be&nbsp;
the Pope or the Emperor had to be crowned by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.320
Pope to officially be the emperor. Of course both&nbsp;
parties supported opposite sides of this argument.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.560
With all this in mind let's look at Mainz in 1159.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.240
The current Archbishop of Mainz Arnold of&nbsp;
Selenhofen was extremely unpopular with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.080
of the local population who promptly assassinated&nbsp;
him. Due to this assasination some of the canons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.160
fled from Mainz to Frankfurt, where they elected&nbsp;
a new archbishop, a nobleman called Christian I.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.080
The people who stayed in Mainz in turn elected&nbsp;
Rudolf of Zähringen, promptly after these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.520
elections the two sides started fighting. The Holy&nbsp;
Roman Emperor at the time, Fredrick Barbarosa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.120
who was also the first Hohenstaufen Emperor, was&nbsp;
kind of sick of all this infighting in Mainz and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.160
dismissed both archbishops while appointing&nbsp;
his own archbishop Conrad of Wittelsbach.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.840
The pope supporters in the comments are&nbsp;
probably yelling hey he can’t do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.480
only the pope can appoint an archbishop. But&nbsp;
Barbarossa was one step ahead, he appointed an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.760
anti-pope and had him dismiss both of the&nbsp;
candidates and appoint Conrad as the archbishop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.240
of Mainz. Of course the actual story is a bit&nbsp;
more complicated, and there’s more reasons to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.360
why Fredrik Barbarosa appointed an anti-pope,&nbsp;
he didn’t just do it because of Mainz but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.960
don’t have time to get into that. Obviously the&nbsp;
current pope wasn’t too happy with all of this.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.320
Also the current ruler of Thuringia, Ludwig&nbsp;
the Second, who was Fredrick's brother in law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.080
wasn’t too happy with this either as&nbsp;
he was a supporter of Christian the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.200
first who had a long standing alliance/friendship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.520
with Ludwig. Ludwig supported the election&nbsp;
that happened in Frankfurt of Christian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.720
as the archbishop of Mainz, hoping to finally&nbsp;
get a favorable ruler in Mainz for Thuringia.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.680
This, however, did not happen as Fredrik Barbarosa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.560
appointed Conrad for some reason and so&nbsp;
Ludwig started plotting against Conrad.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.680
While Mainz and Thuringia continued to clash with&nbsp;
one another the anti-pope who was appointed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.520
Fredrik Barbarossa and who appointed Conrad&nbsp;
as the Archbishop of Mainz died in 1164.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.640
Fredrik Barbarossa promptly appointed a&nbsp;
new anti-pope who, however, wasn’t liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.240
by Conrad. Conrad during this time also started to&nbsp;
become friends with the pope in Rome and all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.600
meant that he fell out of favor with the emperor.&nbsp;
Ludwig of Thuringia quickly took this opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:28.160
and seized Erfurt from Mainz in 1165 while being&nbsp;
supported in this by the Emperor. Conrad seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.880
that the emperor and Ludwig were against him&nbsp;
fled to Rome. Barbarossa then appointed a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.160
archbishop of Mainz, the aforementioned friend of&nbsp;
Ludwig, Christian the First. All of this happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.360
while the pope recognized Conrad to be the&nbsp;
rightful archbishop of Mainz, even though Conrad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.880
was originally appointed to that position by an&nbsp;
antipope. Confused yet? Great, let's continue.&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.120
Ludwig gave Erfurt back to Mainz since&nbsp;
it was now controlled by a family friend.&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.760
Fredrik Barbarossa eventually signed&nbsp;
a peace treaty with the pope in 1177&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.320
ending the whole appointing of anti-popes,&nbsp;
this peace treaty dealt with a lot of stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.640
we don’t have time to get into but one of&nbsp;
its parts required the pope to recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.920
Christian the first as the rightful archbishop&nbsp;
of Mainz. However, Conrad will get the position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:17.840
after Christian dies. This happened in 1183 when&nbsp;
Conrad again became the archbishop of Mainz.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:22.560
Ludwig the Second of Thuringia was dead by&nbsp;
now but his son Ludwig the third upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.280
return of Conrand to Mainz quickly started&nbsp;
plotting against him continuing the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.320
tradition. This plotting and intrigue&nbsp;
had many forms but to give you an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.040
Ludwig started to exert control over Erfurt&nbsp;
by controlling the city's food supply. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.240
see the citizens of Erfurt had to buy food from&nbsp;
the Duchy of Thuringia, which surrounded it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.920
so Ludwig made sure that his subjects would&nbsp;
accept Thuringian coins not coins from Mainz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.760
Meaning Erfurt citizens had to have a supply&nbsp;
of Thuringia coins to buy food. This way Ludwig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.800
could exert control over the city's economy.&nbsp;
This obviously did not sit well with Conrad.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.480
But continuing on with the story, it is now&nbsp;
1184. Conrad is the archbishop of Mainz and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.040
is plotting against Thuringia, Ludwig the&nbsp;
third of Thuringia is plotting against Mainz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.440
and Fredrick Barbarossa is having a&nbsp;
big party to celebrate his victory over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:20.080
another unruly and very powerful subject of the&nbsp;
Holy Roman Empire Henry the Lion, we don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:25.840
time to get into that story, just know that&nbsp;
Fredrik won and made a party to celebrate.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:33.040
This party was called the Diet of Pentecost and it&nbsp;
was held in Mainz. The party was in some sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.080
called the most splendorous occasion yet, and it&nbsp;
“represented a highlight of the knightly way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.440
life” in the high middle ages. There was an entire&nbsp;
city of tents and wooden buildings constructed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:49.520
just for this occasion on the island of Maaraue&nbsp;
just in front of Mainz. There were more than 20k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.280
noblemen and knights present at this party. Even&nbsp;
an entire wooden church was constructed in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:00.160
the main processions of the diet will occur.&nbsp;
There were riding events, there were banquets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.280
there were duels, basically everything you picture&nbsp;
happening at a medieval celebration happened.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.720
During this celebration Emperor Fredrick Barbarosa&nbsp;
concurred upon his two sons a knight hood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:16.160
his eldest son King Henry the VI was already the&nbsp;
king of Germany so getting this knighthood was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:21.280
more symbolic than anything else. Speaking of the&nbsp;
title of the King of Germany, it was usually held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.160
by people before they became the Holy Roman&nbsp;
Emperor. That’s why Henry the VI the eldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.200
son of Fredrik already held the title because&nbsp;
he was expected to become the emperor later on.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.000
There was also a bunch of drama that happened&nbsp;
around Ludwig the Third of Thuringia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Conrad the Archbishop of Mainz during&nbsp;
this Diet of Pentecost but there’s a lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.080
of sources about it and we also don’t have&nbsp;
time to get into that. Just be aware these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:49.120
two people were consistently actively plotting&nbsp;
against each other, even during a celebration.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.800
After the diet ended Emperor Fredrick gave&nbsp;
his son, the newly knighted king Henry the VI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.800 --> 00:10:00.720
a task to finally address the constant feuding&nbsp;
between Thuringia and Mainz. And so Henry called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:06.080
for a royal hearing in Erfurt in the very city&nbsp;
that was at the heart of the feuding. Also it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.480
would be prudent to mention that Ludwig the&nbsp;
Third of Thuringia was Henry the VI cousin.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:20.560
Anyways going back to the royal hearing in Erfurt,&nbsp;
royal hearings rarely consisted of just addressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:25.680
the main problem that was being talked about,&nbsp;
in this case the feud between Ludwig and Conrad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.240
Noblemen from all around the area where the&nbsp;
hearing was held would show up to address the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:35.200
king with whatever problems they had, and some&nbsp;
people would just show up for the event itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.760
to see all the other important people and to meet&nbsp;
the king. It wouldn’t be far-fetched to think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:45.680
as much as a hundred high ranking people and&nbsp;
their entourage showed up in Erfurt that July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.520
when the royal hearing was held.
Also the hearing itself didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:53.600
to be done in one day; it could have&nbsp;
been as much as a month long ordeal.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.320
And it was during one of these July&nbsp;
hearings in Erfurt that the incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.080
I mentioned at the start of the video&nbsp;
happened. We know for sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:07.520
incident happened because there are almost 20&nbsp;
separate mentions of it in medieval sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:13.120
All the mentions of the Erfurt incident are a bit&nbsp;
different but they all present the same story.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.920
The story goes that on the 25th of July a&nbsp;
meeting occurred between King Henry the VI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.280
Conrad the Archbishop of Mainz, Ludwig the 3rd&nbsp;
of Thuringia along with (nobilem Thuringiae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:29.040
civitatem, cum aliquibus principibus et aliis&nbsp;
baronibus.), basically there were also other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:34.080
“noblemen of Thuringia, city officials,&nbsp;
with some princes, and other barons.”&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.600
This meeting either occurred in the&nbsp;
office of the provost of the Erfurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:43.040
Cathedral or at the Petersburg Monastery&nbsp;
just next door. The sources differ on this.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.440
At some point during the meeting, the wooden floor&nbsp;
of the office collapsed under the weight of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:52.080
the people in attendance, this collapsing&nbsp;
floor then hit the wooden floor under it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:57.200
which also collapsed, and under that was&nbsp;
a cesspool filled with human excrements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.800
Almost all the people in attendance fell into this&nbsp;
cesspool, and almost all of them died. The sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:08.640
say that there were four causes of death, first&nbsp;
the fall itself, second falling debris from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:14.800
collapsing floors hitting people, third drowning&nbsp;
in the cesspool, and fourth, the most common one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:20.240
suffocation. This is because decomposing&nbsp;
fecal matter produces Hydrogen sulfide which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:26.560
in very high concentrations can kill a person&nbsp;
in just one breath, and so almost everyone died.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:30.480
Our three main protagonists, due to&nbsp;
which this event was even organized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.480
managed to survive. Conrad the&nbsp;
Archbishop of Mainz and Henry the VI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:38.240
were sitting on a window sill during&nbsp;
the collapse and so didn’t fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:44.480
while Ludwig’s quote “mangled body” was barely&nbsp;
rescued from the cesspool and he later recovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:50.400
from the injuries. Almost, everyone else, however,&nbsp;
including the entourage of the king, died.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:55.040
You might be asking why was there a cesspool&nbsp;
full of fecal matter under a building but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.960
practice was actually quite common. There&nbsp;
were three main ways of getting rid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:04.000
excrements in the middle ages. First, having&nbsp;
a bucket which you occasionally emptied just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:10.000
out on the street or into a nearby river, second,&nbsp;
having your toilets placed over a nearby stream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.680
river, or a moat, and third, digging a hole in&nbsp;
the ground and just filling it up. These holes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:21.200
cesspits or cesspools, were either emptied&nbsp;
everyones in a while or just sealed up and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:26.400
new cesspit was dug out somewhere else. Having&nbsp;
a cesspit under the house most likely in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:31.520
vicinity of the latrines was not uncommon.
Plus collapses like these, during imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:37.040
meetings were also not that uncommon. For&nbsp;
example in 1225 Henry the 7th held a royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.920
meeting in the Nuremberg Castle when suddenly&nbsp;
the staircase leading up to the meeting hall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:48.400
collapsed killing everyone on and under the&nbsp;
staircase, around 40 noblemen perished that day.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.800
Going back to our Erfurt latrine accident though.&nbsp;

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:56.240
Modern retellings of the story talk of 60 to&nbsp;
80 dead people, which is certainly possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.800
but I did not find any specific numbers&nbsp;
mentioned in any of the medieval sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:06.160
so the exact numbers of deaths is unknown.&nbsp;
But it is certain that almost 95% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:12.080
people involved in this incident did die. All of&nbsp;
the people involved were, as already mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:17.680
noblemen, princes, barons, city officials, etc.&nbsp;
People like Count Heinrich von Schwarzburg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:24.240
or Count Friedrich von Abenberg, or Count&nbsp;
Friedrich von Kirchberg, etc. all were now dead.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:28.880
Henry the VI, in a bit of a shock,&nbsp;
quickly departed the city after this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:34.400
incident and the whole feud between Mainz and&nbsp;
Thuringia was never settled and continued on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:40.560
for decades. We know this because in 1232&nbsp;
Konrad, the brother of Ludwig, besieged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:45.200
burned, and plundered the city of Fritzlar&nbsp;
which was owned by the Mainz Archbishopric.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:48.720
All these political machinations&nbsp;
that happened in the 12th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:53.920
along with the unfortunate Erfurt Latrine incident&nbsp;
really did not change many things in the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:59.120
landscape of central HRE. With that said,&nbsp;
however, at least through this close look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:05.360
at one specific region in the high middle ages&nbsp;
one can only imagine all the weird intrigue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:10.080
feuds, and unfortunate accidents that may&nbsp;
have happened all across medieval Europe.

